.TH AENIGMA-KEYS 8 "2026-08-17" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-keys \- generate an RSA keypair for the aenigma service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-keys
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-keys
generates a new 4096-bit RSA private key, encrypted with AES-256, together
with its corresponding public key, for use by the
.I aenigma
service user. The private key is generated first via
.BR openssl (1),
the public key is then derived from it, and both files are placed under
the service's key directory, owned by the
.I aenigma
user, with permissions restricted to that user only.
.PP
The script takes no options or arguments and always generates a key of the
same fixed size into the same fixed location.
.B aenigma-keys
must be run as root, since it creates
.I /var/lib/aenigma
if missing and changes ownership of the generated key files to the
.I aenigma
service account.
.PP
Because the private key is AES-256 encrypted,
.BR openssl (1)
will interactively prompt for a passphrase (and its confirmation) during
generation. This script is not suitable for unattended/non-interactive
use as written.
.PP
If a key pair already exists,
.B aenigma-keys
asks before replacing it, since a new key pair gives the node a new
address. The new pair is written to temporary files in
.I /var/lib/aenigma
and moved into place only when both files are complete, so a failed or
cancelled run leaves the existing pair unchanged and removes the
temporary files.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma
Key storage directory, created if it does not already exist.
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private-key.pem
Generated RSA private key, 4096 bits, AES-256 encrypted, PEM format.
Owned by
.IR aenigma:aenigma ,
mode 0600.
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/public-key.pem
RSA public key derived from the private key, PEM format. Owned by
.IR aenigma:aenigma ,
mode 0600.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; both keys were generated and their ownership/permissions set.
.TP
.B 1
The script was not invoked as root, or replacing the existing key pair
was declined.
.PP
Any failure from
.BR openssl (1)
(for example, a mismatched or empty passphrase) causes the script to abort
immediately, since it runs with
.BR "set -e" ;
the existing key pair is then unchanged.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Replacing the key pair changes the node's address. Peers and clients that
know the old address must be told the new one. Keep a copy of the old pair
if it may be needed again; the script does not keep one.
.IP \(bu 2
The private key passphrase is supplied interactively at the OpenSSL
prompt and is never written to disk, logged, or accepted as a command-line
argument by this script.
.IP \(bu 2
.B umask 077
is set for the duration of the script, so any files it creates default to
owner-only access even before the explicit
.BR chmod (1)
calls run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openssl (1),
.BR umask (2)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.rujas@aenigma.ro>